ACCOUNTING documentation, last updated on 11 Sep 2026

"Accounting" is what the server knows about who a user is: whether it
is identified to the nick it uses, and what host it is shown under.
This file describes how that works now that the server no longer
carries account names, ids or flags of its own, and no longer takes an
ACCOUNT message.  The short version:

  - Every user's host is hidden from the moment it is registered, as a
    cipher of its real address under a key every server shares.  There
    is no opting out, for the user or for the operator.

  - User mode +r means "identified to the nick in use".  The account is
    the nick itself.  A server, a service bot or a net burst puts +r on
    a user and takes it off; the user never does, and a nick change
    takes it off by itself.

  - WHOIS says "is a registered user" (307) about a +r user and nothing
    else; nothing tells an account name because there is none to tell.


THE HIDDEN HOST
===============

register_user() calls hide_hostmask() for every user it introduces --
a client of this server or one another server told it about -- before
the user is announced to the network.  The user gets mode +x, and its
visible host (cli_user()->host) becomes

    xxxxxx.yyyyyy.v4        for an IPv4 address
    xxxxxx.yyyyyy.v6        for an IPv6 address

where xxxxxx.yyyyyy are two 32-bit words rendered in the P10 base64
alphabet (A-Z a-z 0-9, never "[" or "]").  The real host stays in
cli_user()->realhost and the real address in cli_ip(); both are what
G-lines match, what operators are shown in WHOIS (338) and WHO %i, and
what the log records (USERIP shows HIDDEN_IP to everyone but the user
itself, operators included, as it always has).  A client without the
chghost capability is told the host in RPL_HOSTHIDDEN (396) right
after the MOTD.

The cipher is TEA, 32 rounds, over the IPv4 address (with a retry
counter in the first input word so that no output ever carries a
bracket) or over the first 64 bits of the IPv6 address (retrying with
the attempt number as initialisation vector).  The key is the
Security block's virtual_host_key, twelve characters of the same
alphabet, split into two 32-bit words.  ircd/ircd_vhost.c is the
implementation, ircd/test/vhost_t.c the vectors it must reproduce,
tests/vhost.py a Python port for the integration tests.  The scheme
and the derivation are those of IRC-Hispano's ircd (crypt/ipvirtual),
so an operator can reproduce a user's host from its address with an
external tool and the same key; only the outputs that tool could not
have used (a bracket in them) differ.

Two users on the same IPv4 address, or on the same IPv6 /64, hide alike;
so the host is still a handle for a ban.  The IPv4 address is spread
over the whole output, not just the second word: the first word carries
half the key and the retry counter, so nothing about the address can
be read off either half without the key.

Because the host is derived rather than carried, every server derives
it.  The NICK that introduces a user on the wire names the real host
and the real address, as it always did; the receiving server hides it
on its own.  This is why the key is a network-wide setting: two servers
with different keys would show the same user under two hosts, and a
ban set on one would not match on the other.

Bots the server introduces itself (+B, and services +S) keep the host
they were configured with; hide_hostmask() leaves them alone and they
do not carry +x.

The former HOST_HIDING and HIDDEN_HOST features are gone.  HIDDEN_IP
(the address USERIP and WHO %i show for a hidden user, that is, for
every user) and TRUST_USERNAME (drop the ~ from the visible username
of a hidden user, that is, of every user) remain and are described in
readme.features.  What a ban or SILENCE mask can match is unchanged:
the two complete identities ~user@realhost and user@hiddenhost, never
a mix; the account.hiddenhost form is gone with the account.


THE SECURITY BLOCK
==================

    Security {
            virtual_host_key = "AbCdEfGhIjKl";
    };

The block is mandatory.  A configuration without it, or with a key that
is not exactly twelve characters of A-Z a-z 0-9 [ ], is an error: at
start-up the server reports it and refuses to run; on a rehash it
reports it and keeps the key already in force, since a running server
cannot stop hiding hosts.  A new key on a rehash applies to users who
register afterwards; users already on line keep the host they have.
Nothing shows the key: /STATS does not print it and no numeric carries
it.

The same block, with the same key, goes on every server of the network
(see above).  Generate it once -- twelve random characters of the
alphabet -- and treat it as a secret: with it, anyone can turn a host
back into an address by trying addresses, and the address space is
small.


USER MODE +r
============

+r is the mark of a user identified to the nick it is using.  With it
the server keeps cli_user()->account, which is the nick that was
current when +r was granted and nothing else; IsAccount() tests the
flag and cli_account() reads the string, and every place that used to
key on "has an account" -- channel modes +r and +M, user mode +R, the
+R sender exemption -- keys on it still.

Who may set it:

  - A server, with a MODE for the user on its own authority:

        <servernum> M <nick> :+r

    A services package that has verified a password sends this; it is
    what the ACCOUNT message used to be for.  -r the same way.

  - A service bot (+S), local through bot_set_user_mode() or remote
    through the same MODE from the bot's numnick, on any user but an
    operator.  The user sees the MODE as coming from the bot.

  - A net burst: NICK introduces a user with r among its modes.  The
    letter takes no parameter -- the account being the nick, there is
    nothing to say -- and this is a change in the wire format: an
    older ircu reads the token after r as the account name and would
    misparse the rest of the NICK.  A network that identifies users
    cannot keep such a server in it.

Nobody else.  A MODE +r or -r from a user, on itself or on anyone
else, is silently put back the way it was; a user asking to change
another user's modes gets ERR_USERSDONTMATCH as before, and a MODE for
another user from a remote client that is neither a server nor a +S
bot is reported in WALLOPS as a peer misbehaving, as before.

A nick change takes +r away.  set_nick_name() clears the flag and the
account and tells the user with a MODE -r; every server applies the
rule to the NICK it sees, so nothing is put on the wire for it.  A
change of case alone (Foo to FOO) keeps +r and respells the account.
Services that want the new nick identified put +r back.

Set_user_mode() is where all of this is enforced (ircd/s_user.c,
do_user_mode()); set_user_mode_on() is its entry point for a change to
somebody else's modes, and m_mode.c decides who may reach it.


WHAT A SERVICE BOT MAY DO
=========================

bot_set_user_mode(bot, target, "+r") -- include/bot.h.  A +S bot may
change the modes of any user who is not an operator (IsAnOper(): global
or local), and is held to what a user may set on itself plus +r and
-r: +o, +O, +k, +S, +B, +z and -x are dropped from the string, the rest
applies.  That rule is enforced on every server the change passes
through, not only the bot's own, so a peer that claims a bot did more
is not believed.  A server itself (a MODE from a server numeric) is
not held to it: it may change anyone's modes to anything, as one server
trusts another about the users it introduces.

A remote +S bot is one a NICK burst introduced with S among its modes;
only a server puts +S on a user, as before.


WHAT IS GONE
============

  ACCOUNT (AC)            No longer a command.  Received, it is unknown.
  acc_id, acc_flags       Gone from struct User; account[] holds a nick.
  330 RPL_WHOISACCOUNT    Replaced by 307 RPL_WHOISREGNICK, "<nick> :is a
                          registered user", sent for a +r user.
  WHOX %a and the a flag  The field and the match flag are gone; "n"
                          already covers what "a" would say.
  account-notify,         Not advertised, and a CAP REQ for them NAKs.
  account-tag,            JOIN is always the plain form; no @account=
  extended-join           is ever attached.
  SASL                    The sasl capability, AUTHENTICATE, numerics
                          900-908 and the sasl.* netconf keys are gone.
  IAuth R                 Accepted for older iauth programs and treated
                          as D, the account ignored, with a warning to
                          operators (readme.iauth).
  OPMODE +x               Nothing to grant; every user is +x.
  HOST_HIDING,            Removed.  HIDDEN_IP and TRUST_USERNAME stay.
  HIDDEN_HOST


TESTS
=====

  ircd/test/vhost_t.c           the cipher against IRC-Hispano vectors,
                                key validation, the configuration glue
  tests/accounting/             +r from server, bot and burst; nick
                                change; who may change whose modes;
                                the hidden host on hub and leaf; 307
  tests/trust_username/         visible vs. real identity in WHOIS,
                                USERHOST, NAMES, WHO, bans, SILENCE,
                                G-lines
  tests/vhost.py                predicts a client's hidden host
